1
00:00:00,750 --> 00:00:04,070
One line of production code, eight new
assertions.

2
00:00:04,830 --> 00:00:07,170
And before we add that line, four test

3
00:00:07,170 --> 00:00:10,010
groups fail for a reason we can actually
explain.

4
00:00:10,670 --> 00:00:12,370
That's a useful small change.

5
00:00:13,090 --> 00:00:15,350
In this lesson, we'll use cursor to turn

6
00:00:15,350 --> 00:00:18,450
a requirement into evidence, then trace the
paths

7
00:00:18,450 --> 00:00:20,090
a happy path check would miss.

8
00:00:20,790 --> 00:00:22,830
The size of the diff is small.

9
00:00:23,450 --> 00:00:25,430
The thinking is the part we're practicing.

10
00:00:28,050 --> 00:00:30,310
Your turn to make the loop useful.

11
00:00:31,070 --> 00:00:33,390
We're adding one requirement to Operations
Hub.

12
00:00:34,010 --> 00:00:36,730
Every response from the exact health route
should

13
00:00:36,730 --> 00:00:41,330
send Cache-Control, no-store, success,
failure, even

14
00:00:41,330 --> 00:00:42,310
a rejected method.

15
00:00:42,950 --> 00:00:45,750
We'll define the behavior, watch a meaningful
test

16
00:00:45,750 --> 00:00:49,290
fail, make the smallest useful change, and
check

17
00:00:49,290 --> 00:00:50,810
what the actual service sends.

18
00:00:51,590 --> 00:00:53,590
One header gives us plenty to think about.

19
00:00:56,050 --> 00:00:58,010
Quick distinction before we touch code.

20
00:00:58,650 --> 00:01:01,130
No-store tells a compliant cache not to

21
00:01:01,130 --> 00:01:04,510
retain this exchange or reuse the response for
another request.

22
00:01:05,030 --> 00:01:06,190
That's the standard's meaning.

23
00:01:06,810 --> 00:01:08,690
We haven't caught a browser serving an old

24
00:01:08,690 --> 00:01:11,790
health response here, and this header isn't a
privacy guarantee.

25
00:01:12,690 --> 00:01:14,690
Our job in this lesson is narrower.

26
00:01:15,410 --> 00:01:18,030
Make the origin, send the directive
consistently.

27
00:01:20,783 --> 00:01:23,203
We start from the main revision we integrated

28
00:01:23,203 --> 00:01:24,203
in the previous lesson.

29
00:01:24,623 --> 00:01:27,183
The working tree is clean, the health request

30
00:01:27,183 --> 00:01:30,483
still succeeds, and the response includes the
existing

31
00:01:30,483 --> 00:01:31,523
JSON content type.

32
00:01:31,783 --> 00:01:33,383
But there is no cache-control header.

33
00:01:33,823 --> 00:01:35,163
That's the starting observation.

34
00:01:35,603 --> 00:01:38,143
A successful status and a missing new
requirement

35
00:01:38,143 --> 00:01:40,063
can coexist quite happily.

36
00:01:40,743 --> 00:01:42,463
Tests need to know what we're asking for.

37
00:01:44,717 --> 00:01:48,157
The word every is doing real work in this
requirement.

38
00:01:48,997 --> 00:01:50,777
A healthy GET is one path.

39
00:01:51,557 --> 00:01:52,977
HEAD has no response body.

40
00:01:53,677 --> 00:01:55,797
A failed probe takes the exception path.

41
00:01:56,537 --> 00:01:59,897
An unsupported method returns early before the
database probe.

42
00:02:00,597 --> 00:02:02,697
If we only check the happy path, we

43
00:02:02,697 --> 00:02:04,577
can get a green test and still miss

44
00:02:04,577 --> 00:02:05,537
most of the contract.

45
00:02:08,017 --> 00:02:09,117
Here's the discipline.

46
00:02:09,877 --> 00:02:12,697
Change the expectations before changing the
handler.

47
00:02:13,577 --> 00:02:16,797
Ask cursor to add the HTTP assertions, run

48
00:02:16,797 --> 00:02:18,877
that test file, and stop at the result.

49
00:02:19,617 --> 00:02:22,577
A useful red test says the new behavior is
missing.

50
00:02:23,297 --> 00:02:26,497
A syntax error or an unreachable server says
something else.

51
00:02:27,217 --> 00:02:30,097
Read the reason for the failure before
celebrating the color.

52
00:02:32,517 --> 00:02:35,757
The request gives cursor a deliberate stopping
point.

53
00:02:36,237 --> 00:02:39,217
Read the contract, create the lesson branch,
add

54
00:02:39,217 --> 00:02:41,677
the assertions, and run the health test file.

55
00:02:42,257 --> 00:02:43,797
Keep production code unchanged.

56
00:02:44,637 --> 00:02:47,517
This is useful orchestration at a very small
scale.

57
00:02:48,017 --> 00:02:50,537
The agent can inspect and execute while we

58
00:02:50,537 --> 00:02:52,837
keep the decision to implement separate from
the

59
00:02:52,837 --> 00:02:53,817
first evidence review.

60
00:02:56,217 --> 00:02:59,177
Cursor adds the header assertions to the
existing

61
00:02:59,177 --> 00:03:00,577
HTTP tests.

62
00:03:01,137 --> 00:03:04,417
The health test file reports four failures and
two passes.

63
00:03:05,117 --> 00:03:06,917
Now I run the healthy GET case on

64
00:03:06,917 --> 00:03:09,437
its own, so the important detail fits on
screen.

65
00:03:10,057 --> 00:03:12,797
The response arrives, but the cache-control
field

66
00:03:12,797 --> 00:03:13,617
is undefined.

67
00:03:14,277 --> 00:03:16,217
The assertion expected no-store.

68
00:03:16,817 --> 00:03:19,357
This is a real failure against the new
requirement.

69
00:03:21,917 --> 00:03:23,177
One subtlety.

70
00:03:23,777 --> 00:03:27,157
A failing assertion stops the rest of that
test.

71
00:03:27,817 --> 00:03:30,897
It doesn't prove that every later assertion
ran.

72
00:03:31,717 --> 00:03:34,857
In the grouped method test, the first failure

73
00:03:34,857 --> 00:03:38,637
prevents later methods from being checked on
that run.

74
00:03:39,257 --> 00:03:43,657
We inspect where it stopped, then rerun after
the change.

75
00:03:44,177 --> 00:03:47,377
A completion summary can be helpful, but the

76
00:03:47,377 --> 00:03:50,917
test code and the actual output decide what
was checked.

77
00:03:53,517 --> 00:03:56,397
Compare that with a refused connection or a

78
00:03:56,397 --> 00:03:57,197
broken import.

79
00:03:57,817 --> 00:03:59,917
Those failures would tell us to inspect the

80
00:03:59,917 --> 00:04:02,337
test setup before judging the product
behavior.

81
00:04:03,057 --> 00:04:05,877
Here, the request reaches the handler and
fails

82
00:04:05,877 --> 00:04:06,937
on the missing header.

83
00:04:07,537 --> 00:04:09,417
That's why this red result is useful.

84
00:04:09,997 --> 00:04:13,097
We can connect one explicit expectation to one

85
00:04:13,097 --> 00:04:16,217
observable response without asking a model
whether the

86
00:04:16,217 --> 00:04:17,037
code looks right.

87
00:04:19,517 --> 00:04:22,917
Now we can authorize the implementation, keep
the

88
00:04:22,917 --> 00:04:26,237
assertions, satisfy the health contract, and
run type

89
00:04:26,237 --> 00:04:27,997
checking plus the full test suite.

90
00:04:28,657 --> 00:04:30,737
We also ask for a short explanation of

91
00:04:30,737 --> 00:04:31,537
the header's placement.

92
00:04:32,197 --> 00:04:35,517
The interesting decision is which control flow
paths it covers.

93
00:04:36,177 --> 00:04:38,697
There's no prize for making a one-header
requirement

94
00:04:38,697 --> 00:04:40,237
look like a framework migration.

95
00:04:42,817 --> 00:04:45,977
The production change is one line inside the

96
00:04:45,977 --> 00:04:47,517
exact health route branch.

97
00:04:48,077 --> 00:04:50,297
It sets the header before checking the method

98
00:04:50,297 --> 00:04:51,997
and before awaiting the probe.

99
00:04:52,577 --> 00:04:54,817
A rejected method therefore keeps it on the

100
00:04:54,817 --> 00:04:55,577
early return.

101
00:04:56,197 --> 00:04:58,737
If the probe throws, the catch block changes

102
00:04:58,737 --> 00:05:01,617
the status and body while the header remains
set.

103
00:05:02,257 --> 00:05:03,917
Other routes never enter this branch.

104
00:05:06,417 --> 00:05:10,177
Type-checking passes, and the complete suite
reports seven

105
00:05:10,177 --> 00:05:11,077
passing tests.

106
00:05:11,617 --> 00:05:14,817
That count includes the separate database
transaction test.

107
00:05:15,497 --> 00:05:17,857
The health tests now reach the assertions that

108
00:05:17,857 --> 00:05:19,757
the earlier failures had interrupted.

109
00:05:20,437 --> 00:05:22,357
We keep checking the old behavior, too.

110
00:05:23,117 --> 00:05:25,957
Status codes, empty HEAD bodies, the Allow
header,

111
00:05:26,377 --> 00:05:28,957
probe calls, and the routes outside this
change.

112
00:05:31,483 --> 00:05:35,323
Next, restart the actual Operations Hub
service with

113
00:05:35,323 --> 00:05:36,103
the Course Helper.

114
00:05:36,663 --> 00:05:38,823
This makes the runtime check about the changed

115
00:05:38,823 --> 00:05:42,123
handler instead of a process that loaded
yesterday's code.

116
00:05:42,863 --> 00:05:45,023
Then inspect the response headers again.

117
00:05:45,663 --> 00:05:47,663
The same habit matters whether the change is

118
00:05:47,663 --> 00:05:49,403
one line or a major feature.

119
00:05:50,043 --> 00:05:52,963
Know which process and revision your evidence
describes.

120
00:05:55,583 --> 00:05:59,683
The restarted service now sends no-store on a
healthy GET,

121
00:06:00,223 --> 00:06:04,223
Alongside the existing metadata. HEAD keeps
the same directive,

122
00:06:04,723 --> 00:06:08,783
and no response body. POST is still rejected,
and its response

123
00:06:08,783 --> 00:06:11,483
includes both no-store and the allowed
methods.

124
00:06:12,123 --> 00:06:14,703
The demo listing stays available without the
new header.

125
00:06:15,223 --> 00:06:17,743
We can point to the specific responses instead

126
00:06:17,743 --> 00:06:20,443
of treating a green test summary as the whole
story.

127
00:06:22,717 --> 00:06:25,877
The controlled probe tests cover the failure
path

128
00:06:25,877 --> 00:06:27,957
without taking down our real database.

129
00:06:28,797 --> 00:06:31,177
The live requests check the running service.

130
00:06:31,917 --> 00:06:34,937
Neither observation tests every cache in a
deployment.

131
00:06:35,677 --> 00:06:37,097
Our conclusion is precise.

132
00:06:37,777 --> 00:06:40,617
The checked health responses send the required
directive,

133
00:06:41,017 --> 00:06:43,837
and the bounded tests preserve the surrounding
behavior.

134
00:06:44,517 --> 00:06:47,317
That is a useful result we can review and
reproduce.

135
00:06:49,917 --> 00:06:52,417
We keep the reviewed change as a commit,

136
00:06:52,917 --> 00:06:55,297
push the lesson branch, and open the private

137
00:06:55,297 --> 00:06:56,357
course pull request.

138
00:06:57,037 --> 00:07:00,577
The successful CI run identifies the same
change revision.

139
00:07:01,477 --> 00:07:04,037
After merging, we bring main up to date.

140
00:07:04,677 --> 00:07:07,017
The small feature now has a traceable result.

141
00:07:07,637 --> 00:07:10,417
Starting state, failing expectations,
implementation,

142
00:07:11,137 --> 00:07:12,937
checks, and the accepted revision.

143
00:07:13,617 --> 00:07:17,017
The exercise files preserve that trail for
your own practice.

144
00:07:19,517 --> 00:07:23,077
Try this before revealing the answer suppose
the

145
00:07:23,077 --> 00:07:25,657
header is set after awaiting the health probe

146
00:07:25,657 --> 00:07:29,737
a healthy GET test passes. Which two response

147
00:07:29,737 --> 00:07:32,217
paths should you challenge next and why?

148
00:07:33,077 --> 00:07:35,577
Trace the early return and the exception path

149
00:07:35,577 --> 00:07:38,277
in your head. Pause here and explain the

150
00:07:38,277 --> 00:07:41,057
expected behavior before you choose the next
check

151
00:07:49,450 --> 00:07:51,930
Check rejected methods and probe failures.

152
00:07:52,590 --> 00:07:56,310
A rejected method returns before that proposed
header assignment.

153
00:07:56,890 --> 00:07:59,850
A failing probe jumps over it into the catch
block.

154
00:08:00,270 --> 00:08:03,190
Both can miss the directive even when healthy
GET passes.

155
00:08:04,010 --> 00:08:06,350
Our implementation sets the header at the
start

156
00:08:06,350 --> 00:08:09,310
of the health branch, so those paths inherit
it.

157
00:08:09,630 --> 00:08:12,230
The assertions check the response, not just
whether

158
00:08:12,230 --> 00:08:13,350
a certain line exists.

159
00:08:15,783 --> 00:08:19,803
Take the starting snapshot and try the
exercise yourself.

160
00:08:20,683 --> 00:08:24,103
Write the expectation, read the failure, trace
the

161
00:08:24,103 --> 00:08:26,683
paths, and keep the change bounded.

162
00:08:27,423 --> 00:08:30,063
Then explain what your checks establish and
what

163
00:08:30,063 --> 00:08:30,983
they leave open.

164
00:08:31,743 --> 00:08:33,523
That's the habit to carry forward.

165
00:08:34,243 --> 00:08:37,123
Which path would you have forgotten first, the

166
00:08:37,123 --> 00:08:39,263
early return, or the exception?

167
00:08:40,223 --> 00:08:42,543
Your answer tells you where to aim the

168
00:08:42,543 --> 00:08:43,403
next test.
